# Copy to deploy/.env and fill in every value. deploy/.env is gitignored.
#
# Generate the secrets:
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD        openssl rand -base64 32
#   JWT_SECRET               openssl rand -base64 48     (must be >= 32 chars)
#   REALTIME_ENC_KEY         openssl rand -hex 8         (EXACTLY 16 characters)
#   REALTIME_SECRET_KEY_BASE openssl rand -base64 48
#   OTP_PEPPER               openssl rand -base64 48     (>= 32 chars, different per environment)
#   PARTNER_SEAL_KEY         openssl rand -base64 48     (>= 32 chars, different per environment;
#                            OPTIONAL — without it a partner connection can verify an inbound push
#                            and cannot make an outbound pull. Its own secret, not a subkey of the
#                            pepper: rotating the pepper would otherwise force every partner token
#                            to be re-sealed.)
#
# ANON_KEY and SERVICE_ROLE_KEY are JWTs signed with JWT_SECRET. Mint them at
# https://supabase.com/docs/guides/self-hosting#api-keys (the generator runs in
# the browser; nothing leaves the page), or with any JWT tool using:
#   anon:         { "role": "anon",         "iss": "supabase", "iat": ..., "exp": ... }
#   service_role: { "role": "service_role", "iss": "supabase", "iat": ..., "exp": ... }
#
# SERVICE_ROLE_KEY bypasses every RLS policy. It belongs only on the server, in
# the edge functions. It must never reach the browser bundle.

# ── Core ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
JWT_SECRET=
JWT_EXPIRY=3600

ANON_KEY=
SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ── URLs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PUBLIC_URL is where Kong is reachable — the value that becomes
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL in the frontend build.
PUBLIC_URL=https://api.your-domain.de
# APP_URL is where the SPA is served. Auth redirect links are built from it.
APP_URL=https://app.your-domain.de

# ── Mail ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required. GoTrue and the edge functions both send through here, and the
# functions send every one-time code: signup, "Passwort vergessen", invitations,
# email changes. Without it nobody can sign up and a locked-out user has no way
# back in. Port 465 is TLS from the first byte; any other port must offer
# STARTTLS, except staging's catcher `mailpit`. Production uses Resend on 2587
# (deploy/set-resend.sh).
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_SENDER=noreply@your-domain.de
SMTP_SENDER_NAME=El-Professor

# ── One-time codes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The HMAC key the functions hash codes with; the database never sees it.
# At least 32 characters, different in every environment, not quoted.
# deploy.sh refuses a release without it.
OTP_PEPPER=
# Seals the partner bearer token so an outbound pull can replay it. Leave
# empty until the Reidey connection is in use; nothing reads it before then.
PARTNER_SEAL_KEY=
# Where an outbound partner call may go. CONFIGURATION, never taken from a
# request: these functions share a Docker network with Reidey's database and
# ours, so a destination from a request body would be an SSRF into our own
# network. Validated by _shared/partner-endpoint.ts — https only, a public
# host, no credentials, port 443, and no path.
PARTNER_REIDEY_BASE_URL=https://reidey.de

# ── Google sign-in ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off unless GOOGLE_SIGNIN_ENABLED is exactly true; empty or false keeps it off,
# deploy.sh refuses any other value, and the auth container will not start GoTrue
# with one. Switching it on also opens GoTrue's own
# signup, which Kong keeps closed on /signup, /otp and /magiclink. The kill
# switch is false here plus a redeploy.
# The client's authorised redirect URI is PUBLIC_URL + /auth/v1/callback. Write
# the secret on the host, never in chat.
#
# Staging and production may share one Google OAuth client (owner decision G3,
# 17.09.2026), and then GOOGLE_CLIENT_SHARED=true must stand in both .env files.
# deploy.sh refuses the same GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID in both without it, so sharing is
# a written decision and not an accident of editing; what that catches is the
# other environment's id pasted in here while no opt-in was written. The opt-in
# on one side only is refused as well. Empty or false means "not shared", and
# any other value is refused. It does not catch a wholesale copy of the other
# environment's .env once both carry the opt-in — PROJECT_NAME does that.
# Write both files in the same sitting: one side alone fails the other side's
# next release. What sharing means: staging runs a copy of production's
# accounts, so one client is one consent screen and one token audience across
# both environments.
GOOGLE_SIGNIN_ENABLED=false
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SHARED=false

# ── Realtime ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EXACTLY 16 characters. Realtime hands this string straight to
# :crypto.crypto_one_time(:aes_128_ecb, key, ...) and AES-128 takes a 16-byte
# key — the value is used as-is, not hex-decoded. A longer key is not a stronger
# key, it is a container that will not start: `Erlang error: ... Bad key size`.
REALTIME_ENC_KEY=
REALTIME_SECRET_KEY_BASE=

# ── Sharing the box ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This VPS also runs the ridy project. Kong binds to loopback on this port —
# change it if 8000 is already taken (check with: ss -tlnp | grep :8000).
# Whatever you set here, the reverse proxy in front must point at the same port.
KONG_BIND_PORT=8000

# Postgres memory ceiling. Capped so a Fahrtenbuch import cannot make the kernel
# OOM-kill the other project's database. Check real headroom with `free -h` and
# `docker stats` before raising it.
DB_MEM_LIMIT=4G

# ── Routing (optional, off by default) ─────────────────────────────────────
# The travel check of the Fahrtenanalyse can measure the road between two points
# with an OSRM container of our own (the `osrm` service in docker-compose.yml).
# It starts only when COMPOSE_PROFILES names osrm (production: osrm; staging:
# staging,osrm) AND its map has been built with deploy/osrm-build-data.sh, which
# is a long job and tens of gigabytes of memory for Germany and is never part of a
# release; deploy.sh refuses a release with the profile on and no build.
# The container's memory ceiling. About 9 GiB is what OSRM's own wiki scales to
# for Germany; check `docker stats` after the first week and move it.
#OSRM_MEM_LIMIT=12G
